      "text": "To solve this problem, we start by identifying the lengths of the legs of the right triangle. The legs are given as \\(\\log_4 27\\) and \\(\\log_2 9\\).\n\nFirst, we convert \\(\\log_4 27\\) to a base 2 logarithm. Using the change of base formula, we have:\n\\[\n\\log_4 27 = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{\\log_2 4} = \\frac{\\log_2 27}{2}\n\\]\nSince \\(27 = 3^3\\), we can write \\(\\log_2 27\\) as:\n\\[\n\\log_2 27 = \\log_2 (3^3) = 3 \\log_2 3\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n\\log_4 27 = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2} = \\frac{3}{2} \\log_2 3\n\\]\n\nNext, we convert \\(\\log_2 9\\) to a base 2 logarithm:\n\\[\n\\log_2 9 = \\log_2 (3^2) = 2 \\log_2 3\n\\]\n\nNow, we use the Pythagorean theorem to find the length of the hypotenuse \\(h\\), which is given by:\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{(\\log_4 27)^2 + (\\log_2 9)^2}\n\\]\nSubstituting the values we found:\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{3}{2} \\log_2 3\\right)^2 + (2 \\log_2 3)^2}\n\\]\nSimplify each term inside the square root:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{3}{2} \\log_2 3\\right)^2 = \\frac{9}{4} (\\log_2 3)^2\n\\]\n\\[\n(2 \\log_2 3)^2 = 4 (\\log_2 3)^2\n\\]\nAdding these together:\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4} (\\log_2 3)^2 + 4 (\\log_2 3)^2} = \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{9}{4} + 4\\right) (\\log_2 3)^2} = \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{9}{4} + \\frac{16}{4}\\right) (\\log_2 3)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4} (\\log_2 3)^2} = \\frac{5}{2} \\log_2 3\n\\]\n\nWe need to compute \\(4^h\\):\n\\[\n4^h = 4^{\\frac{5}{2} \\log_2 3} = (2^2)^{\\frac{5}{2} \\log_2 3} = 2^{2 \\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\log_2 3} = 2^{5 \\log_2 3}\n\\]\nUsing the property of logarithms \\(a^{\\log_b c} = c^{\\log_b a}\\), we get:\n\\[\n2^{5 \\log_2 3} = (2^{\\log_2 3})^5 = 3^5 = 243\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{243}\n\\]",
